On Jul 8, 6:00 am, Derek Ray <moot@just_a_spamtrap_anyway.com> wrote:

> He went for very fast real-time ascensions and averaged 8.5 hours of
> play per day.  This is within the realm of possibility for a student,
> either college or high school, who is home on summer vacation and
> chooses to try for a very high "most ascensions".

So someone chose to make playing Nethack their full-time job for a
month. I suppose that's not unprecedented . But someone with the time,
resources, and desire to play that much would likely beat any game, no
matter how difficult, and likely also burn out from so much play, no
matter how interesting the game was.

> This doesn't have a precedent in devnull, either, which has been around
> a lot longer than that -- and the trophies are the same as the ones for
> devnull.  Marvin has done a grand-slam with bells, I believe.  But to
> say that there are suddenly three more people capable of playing at his
> level doesn't imply that there's a noticeable increase of skill in the
> playerbase?  C'mon.

Sure, there's an increase in skill, at the elite level. I still don't
think that makes the game "easy". Three more people at a very highly-
skilled level compared to thousands of players worldwide? That's not a
great example. Looking at overall stats on various public servers over
a period of time, perhaps in the four years since 3.4.3 came out,
might be a better way to prove your point.

> No, what it comes down to is that highly skilled players really DO find
> the game easier than they used to -- because the level of skill has
> increased to the point where the game really _is_ easy.  All the
> problems have a defined solution, and the solution is always readily
> available; since Nethack is a turn-based game, the only thing left is to
> execute consistently.

I actually don't disagree with you that the game could use
improvements to make it more challenging. I prefer the route of
conducts for some things,  but can also see the appeal of hard-wiring
more difficulty into the game. For example, I remember when one could
deal with the Riders trivially by teleporting them or their corpses
away. Changing that behavior was a big and unpleasant surprise to me,
but I think a very definite improvement.

I guess I just haven't thought about the powers of the Eye much
because I almost never use it; I rarely play wizards, and I don't wish
for quest artifacts. It does seem overpowered compared to the others.

> Just because you have chosen not to use the knowledge available to you
> doesn't mean that the knowledge wasn't present or that others haven't
> done so.  For example, where do you think the Cabal came from?

I did specifically say that I was speaking only for myself. But I
can't be the only one who's had success without spending a lot of time
on IRC or watching games.

> Also, being able to call your deaths "carelessness" really demonstrates
> my point quite well; when the game is hard, the game beats you.  You get
> in a situation where you don't know what to do, where you don't have the
> resources, where you're just overrun.  When the game is easy, you die
> only because you're careless.

I don't die only because of carelessness; I think a lot about my
games. Though once I've got my ascension kit things get considerably
easier, I never take an ascension for granted; the existence of
Pestilence, Demogorgon, and non-genocideable nasties like Archons
ensures that.

Again, no argument from me that the game could use improvement, and
that for some players it is indeed "easy". I just bristle at the
overall experience being called "easy". The whimsies of the RNG means
that even if most problems have defined solutions, you simply may not
be able to solve them given the available resources, and must find
more creative ways out of your situation.

